---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import UrlEncoder from '../../components/UrlEncoder.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="URL Encoder / Decoder" description="Encode special characters for safe use in URLs, or decode a URL-encoded string back to plain text.">
  <UrlEncoder client:load slot="tool" />
  <h2>Why URL encoding matters</h2>
  <p>Characters like spaces, &, and ? have special meaning in URLs. Encoding replaces them with percent-escaped equivalents so query parameters don't break.</p>

  <h2>Common URL Encoding Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Encoding the entire URL instead of just parameter values</strong>, breaking the URL's structural characters (like the protocol's colon-slash-slash) that shouldn't be encoded.</li>
    <li><strong>Double-encoding an already-encoded string</strong>, producing garbled output when decoded incorrectly downstream.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This URL Encoder</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Safely including a value with special characters in a URL query parameter</li>
    <li>Decoding a URL-encoded string to read its actual content</li>
    <li>Debugging a broken URL caused by unencoded special characters</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>URL Encoding FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What's the difference between encodeURIComponent and encodeURI?</strong> <code>encodeURIComponent</code> escapes characters like <code>&amp;</code> and <code>=</code> that have special meaning in a full URL, making it the right choice for encoding individual query parameter values; <code>encodeURI</code> leaves those characters alone since it's meant for encoding a complete URL.</p>
  <p><strong>Are spaces encoded as %20 or +?</strong> Percent-encoding uses <code>%20</code>; the <code>+</code> character for spaces is specific to the <code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code> format used in query strings and form submissions, not general URL encoding.</p>
</ToolLayout>
